97
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.720
Fast fact number one.

98
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:12.360
You might remember, because we shared this earlier, that NBC ordered 28 episodes for season

99
00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:12.640
five.

100
00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.160
It was our longest season.

101
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.380
I remember, Jenna, when we wrapped season five, we all got sick.

102
00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:20.580
Oh, yeah.

103
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.540
Like, we all got the flu or something.

104
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.880
Because I remember the next week, like, texting a few people.

105
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:25.780
I'm like, I have a cold.

106
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.040
And Kate was like, yeah, me too.

107
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.880
Like, I think our bodies were just like...

108
00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:35.880
A standard network television season usually runs on television from September to May.

109
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.720
We work from July to March, right?

110
00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.640
So that we can get the episodes ready.

111
00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:49.380
And a standard television schedule is usually where you do three weeks on, one week off of

112
00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:49.860
filming.

113
00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.840
So usually a show will take one hiatus week a month.

114
00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.780
But the writers always continue to work for that week.

115
00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:02.560
That week off is meant so that the writers and the producer, they have a sort of catch-up

116
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:02.920
week.

117
00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.280
Because they're constantly writing, prepping, editing, delivering the episodes.

118
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.380
They would have these episodes in multiple forMs.

119
00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.700
They're, like, doing an outline for one, but they're writing another.

120
00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.180
They're punching up another one.

121
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.200
They're doing post-editing on another one.

122
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.360
They've got, like, five episodes going at once.

123
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.100
We would just, like, be like, what are we filming this week?

124
00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:30.720
This year, with this crazy 28-episode season, Steve Carell also had a commitment to do a

125
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:31.080
film.

126
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.680
I think it was Get Smart.

127
00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:38.380
And he needed, like, a six- to eight-week break from the show in the middle of that July

128
00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.120
to March filming schedule so he could film Get Smart.

129
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.700
How did we ever do that?

130
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:48.000
In order to do that, we had to film 19 episodes in a row.

131
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.980
That's when we got sick.

132
00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.900
That's when we got sick.

133
00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.680
I just have a memory of a bunch of us getting a cold bug.

134
00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.340
That was it.

135
00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:57.980
Yeah.

136
00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.780
So Frame Toby was right in the middle of this crazy 19-episode schedule.

137
00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:04.840
Oy, oy, oy.

138
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.060
And here's why I think this is significant.

139
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:15.420
It's really crazy to me that they decided to bring Toby back in the middle of this 19-episode

140
00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.240
stretch because, remember, he was now our showrunner because Greg was doing Parks and

141
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:20.460
Rec.

142
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.240
So Paul Lieberstein is now showrunning and acting in an episode.

143
00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:27.560
Again, because they gave him that break.

144
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.480
And now they're like, sorry, bud.

145
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:31.040
You need to be Toby again.

146
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.200
You're back in the mix.

147
00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.280
So I reached out to Jen Salata, who was running the show with him.

148
00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.380
And I said, do you know why you did that?

149
00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.640
Did you just want Paul to never sleep?

150
00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.060
Like, what was your plan there?

151
00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:47.980
And she said she can't remember exactly the reasoning, but because Holly had left, they

152
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.640
were either going to have to cast someone new or bring Toby back.

153
00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:55.120
And that dynamic between Toby and Michael was so beloved.

154
00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:00.180
She said as a fan of the show, she wanted Toby to come back to that role.

155
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.360
As a co-showrunner, she was freaked out at the idea of Paul having to wear both hats.

156
00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.320
But she said he did it so well.

157
00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:15.200
And Paul said he doesn't remember sleeping a lot, but also he enjoyed doing it.

158
00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:17.400
You know, he liked his time on set with us.

159
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:22.280
Well, I love Toby coming back just because watching Michael spiral is delicious.

160
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.220
This is one of my favorite episodes of the season for that reason.

161
00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:29.760
So thank you, Paul, for doing that.

162
00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:30.680
For Paul, but thank you.

