1287
00:54:09.310 --> 00:54:11.970
Also, what about that question, who took these pictures?

1288
00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:13.130
That's in the script.

1289
00:54:13.330 --> 00:54:14.190
Oh, it is.

1290
00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:14.590
Yeah.

1291
00:54:14.610 --> 00:54:15.630
That's a funny line.

1292
00:54:15.890 --> 00:54:17.490
The improv moment is the monkey.

1293
00:54:17.790 --> 00:54:18.070
Okay.

1294
00:54:18.390 --> 00:54:24.270
Now the police arrive and they are going to look into this possible narcotic situation.

1295
00:54:24.790 --> 00:54:26.330
Pam doesn't know anything about it.

1296
00:54:26.770 --> 00:54:27.890
Dwight's going to take charge.

1297
00:54:28.240 --> 00:54:30.230
And then Michael starts freaking out.

1298
00:54:30.390 --> 00:54:30.690
Yes.

1299
00:54:30.830 --> 00:54:34.150
And then they realize it's a Ziploc of maybe like, what, a caprese salad?

1300
00:54:34.510 --> 00:54:34.910
Yeah.

1301
00:54:35.090 --> 00:54:38.210
It looks like there's definitely some cheese in there.

1302
00:54:38.410 --> 00:54:38.910
Mm-hmm.

1303
00:54:39.070 --> 00:54:40.330
And some salad dressing.

1304
00:54:40.850 --> 00:54:41.230
Yeah.

1305
00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:46.970
I'll have you know the police officers were played by Patrick Fawcett and Michael Harity,

1306
00:54:47.210 --> 00:54:49.310
and neither of them have been on Monk.

1307
00:54:49.450 --> 00:54:50.170
I checked.

1308
00:54:50.450 --> 00:54:52.330
Oh, good call to check, though.

1309
00:54:52.750 --> 00:54:57.270
Also, can we talk about how when the police first arrived, Creed stood up?

1310
00:54:57.650 --> 00:54:57.890
Oh.

1311
00:54:58.170 --> 00:54:59.870
As if he was going to be handcuffed.

1312
00:55:00.070 --> 00:55:02.110
And then put his hands behind his head.

1313
00:55:02.350 --> 00:55:02.590
Yeah.

1314
00:55:02.810 --> 00:55:07.190
And then his talking head where he's like, just keep talking to me until the police leave.

1315
00:55:07.430 --> 00:55:08.090
I love it.

1316
00:55:08.350 --> 00:55:08.930
So good.

1317
00:55:09.110 --> 00:55:10.310
I love it so much.

1318
00:55:10.730 --> 00:55:11.910
We got some fan mail.

1319
00:55:12.090 --> 00:55:12.510
Brooke L.

1320
00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:17.490
said that Toby seemed very concerned about police officers searching his desk.

1321
00:55:17.910 --> 00:55:21.890
Could this be because they might find evidence of him being the Scranton Strangler?

1322
00:55:22.330 --> 00:55:22.810
Oh.

1323
00:55:23.470 --> 00:55:24.250
I don't know.

1324
00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:31.930
Well, at 16 minutes, 27 seconds, we have a very rare side-by-side talking head.

1325
00:55:32.210 --> 00:55:33.050
So rare.

1326
00:55:33.290 --> 00:55:34.390
How did they get through it?

1327
00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:35.430
I don't know.

1328
00:55:35.670 --> 00:55:37.890
Michael and Toby side-by-side.

1329
00:55:38.130 --> 00:55:38.930
I know.

1330
00:55:39.050 --> 00:55:42.430
I would have loved to have been a fly on a wall on that talking head because you know.

1331
00:55:42.630 --> 00:55:43.750
I looked for the bloopers.

1332
00:55:43.910 --> 00:55:47.130
I was like, surely, surely there's something on the blooper reel.

1333
00:55:47.130 --> 00:55:47.350
I knew.

1334
00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:48.130
Yes.

1335
00:55:48.130 --> 00:55:48.570
Yes.

1336
00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:50.590
Because I was like, I know these guys.

1337
00:55:50.850 --> 00:55:52.290
I know Steve and Paul together.

1338
00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:57.710
And they were as tickled by the Toby-Michael relationship as the audience was, as we all

1339
00:55:57.710 --> 00:55:58.070
were.

1340
00:55:58.310 --> 00:55:58.770
Yes.

1341
00:55:58.850 --> 00:56:00.310
And they would mess with each other.

1342
00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:05.270
You remember in a scene, there's a blooper where Toby has to hand Michael a piece of paper

1343
00:56:05.270 --> 00:56:08.590
and Steve and him start cracking up because Paul wrote, f*** you on it?

1344
00:56:08.770 --> 00:56:09.170
Yes.

1345
00:56:10.330 --> 00:56:15.250
So I know they were giving it to each other, and I wish there were some bloopers from this.

1346
00:56:15.350 --> 00:56:17.130
There is footage somewhere for sure.

1347
00:56:17.690 --> 00:56:21.910
Well, listen, in the annex, Ryan and Kelly are making out again.

1348
00:56:22.230 --> 00:56:24.810
Oh, it's such a sloppy makeout, too.

1349
00:56:25.010 --> 00:56:25.710
It is.

1350
00:56:25.910 --> 00:56:30.310
Not that a tidy makeout would have made it any better at work.

1351
00:56:30.990 --> 00:56:31.470
Exactly.

1352
00:56:31.970 --> 00:56:33.550
Well, Ryan wants out.

1353
00:56:33.890 --> 00:56:35.330
He doesn't want a sloppy makeout.

1354
00:56:35.450 --> 00:56:36.550
He doesn't want a tidy one.

1355
00:56:36.550 --> 00:56:41.990
And he's going to Thailand with some friends from high school, a high school.

1356
00:56:42.430 --> 00:56:46.410
You know, he has to break up with her because if he doesn't, he'll always resent her.

1357
00:56:46.510 --> 00:56:49.870
If he didn't go, he would like them to be adults about it.

1358
00:56:50.090 --> 00:56:51.830
Maybe they could have sex one more time.

1359
00:56:51.990 --> 00:56:53.330
And does she have any cash?

1360
00:56:53.650 --> 00:56:56.790
Then let's be adults about it, but we could also have sex one more time.

1361
00:56:57.150 --> 00:56:58.590
That's such a dude move.

1362
00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:00.630
I feel like I've heard that so many times.

1363
00:57:00.890 --> 00:57:01.530
I know.

1364
00:57:01.830 --> 00:57:03.450
Do you want to have some goodbye sex?

1365
00:57:03.770 --> 00:57:04.150
No.

1366
00:57:06.550 --> 00:57:08.290
Get your sorry ass out of here.

1367
00:57:08.890 --> 00:57:10.190
Yeah, exactly.

1368
00:57:10.810 --> 00:57:11.110
Yeah.

1369
00:57:11.430 --> 00:57:13.230
Fan question from Gracie R.

1370
00:57:13.470 --> 00:57:19.830
When Ryan is telling Kelly he's going to Thailand, why are they both wearing different clothes?

1371
00:57:20.150 --> 00:57:21.730
I'll tell you, Gracie.

1372
00:57:21.910 --> 00:57:30.810
Because this scene was originally supposed to be the tag at the end of the episode, and it was meant to be a new workday.

1373
00:57:30.810 --> 00:57:36.350
So in the script, this scene was not during the same day that we were framing Toby.

1374
00:57:36.650 --> 00:57:37.650
It was the next day.

1375
00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:45.950
But in editing, they moved it up, and we are going to end instead with a delicious Dwight talking head.

1376
00:57:46.150 --> 00:57:47.030
We'll get to it.

1377
00:57:47.030 --> 00:57:52.050
But first, Jim needs to show Pam what he's done.

1378
00:57:52.610 --> 00:57:53.050
That's right.

1379
00:57:53.390 --> 00:57:54.750
Jim is nervous but excited.

1380
00:57:54.990 --> 00:57:57.650
He is, as my daughter says, nervous-sided.

1381
00:57:58.010 --> 00:57:58.370
Mm-hmm.

1382
00:57:58.490 --> 00:58:01.230
And she's like, why are we stopping at your parents' house?

1383
00:58:01.430 --> 00:58:03.870
And he says, I bought it.

1384
00:58:04.010 --> 00:58:08.490
And then Jenna, at 18 minutes, two seconds, Jim says, let's go inside.

1385
00:58:08.490 --> 00:58:09.530
I'll show you inside.

1386
00:58:09.870 --> 00:58:10.330
What?

1387
00:58:10.630 --> 00:58:12.750
Has Pam never been inside the house?

1388
00:58:12.750 --> 00:58:14.470
We got so much mail about that.

1389
00:58:14.750 --> 00:58:15.630
People wrote in.

1390
00:58:15.830 --> 00:58:22.050
They said, if Pam knew they were at Jim's parents' house when they pulled up, wouldn't that mean she's been there before and she's probably been inside?

1391
00:58:22.330 --> 00:58:24.830
You would hope Pam has been inside his parents' house.

1392
00:58:24.930 --> 00:58:25.890
They've been dating for a while.

1393
00:58:25.970 --> 00:58:27.050
They live in the same town.

1394
00:58:27.350 --> 00:58:31.710
Yeah, and it's interesting because his parents have moved out, but they've left some furniture.

1395
00:58:31.990 --> 00:58:34.590
Also, how rough were they on this house?

1396
00:58:34.870 --> 00:58:35.590
I know.

1397
00:58:36.110 --> 00:58:38.970
And Jim says, I was helping my mom out.

1398
00:58:39.410 --> 00:58:40.330
Where's his dad?

1399
00:58:40.530 --> 00:58:41.450
I don't know.

1400
00:58:41.450 --> 00:58:42.750
I have a lot of questions.

1401
00:58:43.170 --> 00:58:44.590
There were a lot of questions.

1402
00:58:45.190 --> 00:58:45.810
Rachel M.

1403
00:58:45.970 --> 00:58:46.810
and Megan L.

1404
00:58:47.170 --> 00:58:48.770
said, question for Jenna.

1405
00:58:49.030 --> 00:58:56.590
In this episode, when Jim is showing Pam his parents' house, I was wondering what went into how you decided to play this scene?

1406
00:58:56.870 --> 00:59:03.050
Because she seems really worried and unexcited, but then in the end, she says she loves it.

1407
00:59:03.470 --> 00:59:09.670
Any input you could give about how you decided to play this or what the director told you would be super appreciated.

1408
00:59:09.670 --> 00:59:17.430
Well, guys, we've already discussed my personal opinions on someone buying someone else a house without telling them.

1409
00:59:17.430 --> 00:59:28.790
So this was a little bit of a mental gymnastics for me to get Pam to the place where she's going to say she loves it and where she says that line.

1410
00:59:28.790 --> 00:59:36.710
I mean, you bought me a house because it was like not how I would react as Jenna.

1411
00:59:36.970 --> 00:59:41.710
You know, I had to really play the character and play the character's truth.

1412
00:59:41.710 --> 00:59:50.530
And in the script, I was being told that my character's truth was that she loved this gesture and it made her feel loved.

1413
00:59:51.110 --> 00:59:51.290
Yeah.

1414
00:59:51.570 --> 00:59:53.270
Well, I mean, I think we often did that.

1415
00:59:53.390 --> 00:59:55.350
Obviously, we had to play the truth for the character.

1416
00:59:55.690 --> 01:00:01.310
I mean, I hope you guys know I'm not very much like Angela Martin and how I would react to things.

1417
01:00:01.310 --> 01:00:10.890
But it made sense to me, Jenna, for Pam, you know, and Jim made the garage into this art studio and he's really putting down some roots with her.

1418
01:00:11.130 --> 01:00:17.690
And I think Pam wanted a family and she wanted all of these things that Jim is offering her.

1419
01:00:17.890 --> 01:00:19.210
She had wanted that.

1420
01:00:19.210 --> 01:00:30.270
Yeah, you know, I had thought a little bit back on The Talking Head where Pam discusses what her perfect home would look like, how she always wanted a home with a terrace.

1421
01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:31.290
Mm-hmm.

1422
01:00:31.510 --> 01:00:36.510
You know, picking her home seemed like something that Pam was attached to.

1423
01:00:36.690 --> 01:00:38.450
So I did bring that up.

1424
01:00:38.670 --> 01:00:45.350
I said, you know, we've established that Pam has daydreamed about what her home would look like.

1425
01:00:45.350 --> 01:00:49.050
And now this person has taken her out of the process.

1426
01:00:49.370 --> 01:00:52.790
You brought that up to the writers at the time of when we were filming?

1427
01:00:53.170 --> 01:00:53.370
Yeah.

1428
01:00:53.690 --> 01:01:01.390
And so I was saying, like, help me understand why Pam now is so happy and so excited.

1429
01:01:01.650 --> 01:01:10.910
And, Angela, we got to exactly what you just said, which was that it was a vulnerable thing for Pam to give up the art school track.

1430
01:01:11.350 --> 01:01:14.730
And she did that because of two reasons.

1431
01:01:15.350 --> 01:01:20.650
One, graphic design wasn't what she meant she wanted to do as an artiSt.

1432
01:01:21.030 --> 01:01:23.030
And two, she's ready.

1433
01:01:23.290 --> 01:01:35.570
She's very, very ready to start living this married life, to be in the season of family and marriage with this man that she's been waiting years for.

1434
01:01:35.830 --> 01:01:41.910
And so this home is her fast track to that season of her life.

1435
01:01:42.050 --> 01:01:43.130
And she's just ready.

1436
01:01:44.030 --> 01:01:46.670
And, you know, they're going to get to fix it up together.

1437
01:01:46.670 --> 01:01:50.230
So there's still that element of nesting that's going to get to happen.

1438
01:01:50.490 --> 01:01:55.490
And so that's kind of what I was thinking was like, I'm ready.

1439
01:01:55.490 --> 01:01:56.190
Yeah.

1440
01:01:56.410 --> 01:02:05.890
And you have to think, when Pam talked about her dream house with this tower and the terrace and all of that, she was in an unhappy relationship.

1441
01:02:05.990 --> 01:02:10.270
When you're in a place you feel stuck in life or you're unhappy, you daydream.

1442
01:02:10.490 --> 01:02:15.230
And you daydream about these fantastical things and their coping mechanisMs.

1443
01:02:15.230 --> 01:02:20.810
And then when you're with someone you really love, you don't need the fantasy anymore.

1444
01:02:21.050 --> 01:02:22.930
That's such a good point, Angela.

1445
01:02:23.230 --> 01:02:24.610
You're just ready to have a life.

1446
01:02:24.750 --> 01:02:26.690
And she's ready to have that life.

1447
01:02:26.970 --> 01:02:27.310
Yeah.

1448
01:02:27.310 --> 01:02:30.050
Now, we did get some mail about that garage.

1449
01:02:30.870 --> 01:02:31.430
Katie C.

1450
01:02:31.570 --> 01:02:32.290
and Chris G.

1451
01:02:32.530 --> 01:02:32.790
said,

1452
01:02:32.930 --> 01:02:37.350
When Jim is showing Pam his parents' house, he says the garage has great lighting.

1453
01:02:37.770 --> 01:02:43.770
He then proceeds to open a windowless garage door, revealing a completely windowless garage space.

1454
01:02:44.070 --> 01:02:45.650
Where's the great lighting, Jim?

1455
01:02:45.990 --> 01:02:46.370
Where?

1456
01:02:46.770 --> 01:02:47.250
Okay.

1457
01:02:47.430 --> 01:02:48.690
Well, I thought that was really funny.

1458
01:02:49.090 --> 01:02:50.270
That is really funny.

1459
01:02:50.270 --> 01:02:55.030
I want you guys to know that I have a garage that we use as a home office.

1460
01:02:55.610 --> 01:02:58.990
And for a long time, we would just raise the door.

1461
01:02:59.130 --> 01:03:01.850
And then there would be amazing light, you know?

1462
01:03:02.150 --> 01:03:02.550
Yeah.

1463
01:03:02.590 --> 01:03:05.950
So it did get great light, but you had to raise the door.

1464
01:03:06.190 --> 01:03:10.310
And then years later, we actually just removed the door because we're like,

1465
01:03:10.390 --> 01:03:12.130
this is never going to be a garage again.

1466
01:03:12.370 --> 01:03:17.050
You know, we've sort of made it into this little home office and put an actual sliding glass door.

1467
01:03:17.330 --> 01:03:18.870
But I get it.

1468
01:03:20.270 --> 01:03:21.610
You just have to open it up.

1469
01:03:21.750 --> 01:03:26.170
But he does present it like this garage has great lighting,

1470
01:03:26.830 --> 01:03:33.210
as if the other garages that you open, the whole side of the building, wouldn't have great lighting.

1471
01:03:33.330 --> 01:03:38.630
He's presenting as if it's like a special feature that kind of applies to every garage

1472
01:03:38.630 --> 01:03:43.310
that doesn't have like a giant tree planted in front of it, which it wouldn't because it's a garage.

1473
01:03:43.610 --> 01:03:43.830
Right.

1474
01:03:43.990 --> 01:03:46.370
Most garages are just boxes with no windows.

1475
01:03:47.150 --> 01:03:50.630
I have to tell you two things about shooting these scenes at the house.

1476
01:03:50.870 --> 01:03:59.730
The scene where Jim is trying to take the clown painting off the wall for Pam is one of the hardest I've ever laughed on the show.

1477
01:03:59.890 --> 01:04:01.610
It is the second hardest time.

1478
01:04:01.810 --> 01:04:07.590
The first was when we did the scene in Dinner Party where Michael's showing us the tiny TV on the wall.

1479
01:04:08.470 --> 01:04:09.730
This is the second.

1480
01:04:10.670 --> 01:04:16.250
Every time John tried to lift the painting on the wall, I could not stop laughing.

1481
01:04:16.470 --> 01:04:17.750
I don't know what it was.

1482
01:04:18.150 --> 01:04:21.050
Tears, tears, choking on our laughter.

1483
01:04:21.430 --> 01:04:22.710
We laughed so hard.

1484
01:04:22.970 --> 01:04:25.930
I looked for it in the bloopers and it's not there.

1485
01:04:25.970 --> 01:04:27.750
And I'm so upset it's not there.

1486
01:04:27.910 --> 01:04:35.170
It took us like an hour to shoot that tiny moment of him trying to take the painting off the wall.

1487
01:04:35.170 --> 01:04:38.170
John is also so funny at physical comedy.

1488
01:04:38.550 --> 01:04:38.970
Yes.

1489
01:04:39.210 --> 01:04:43.670
I laughed out loud when he peeled back that fake wood paneling and it kind of startled him.

1490
01:04:44.030 --> 01:04:44.350
Yes.

1491
01:04:44.590 --> 01:04:51.150
So then at the end of the scene in the garage, when we're hugging, I improvised the line,

1492
01:04:51.310 --> 01:04:52.690
what about the clown?

1493
01:04:53.030 --> 01:04:54.630
And it made John laugh.

1494
01:04:54.770 --> 01:04:57.790
And that's why he's like burying his head into my neck.

1495
01:04:57.790 --> 01:05:03.430
And then when he said, yeah, I can't do anything about that, it made me laugh.

1496
01:05:03.470 --> 01:05:06.050
And so I'm like smushing my face into his shoulder.

1497
01:05:06.350 --> 01:05:07.730
You can totally tell we're breaking.

1498
01:05:08.050 --> 01:05:13.990
And that's why it was because we were still, anytime we brought up the clown painting, we lost it.

1499
01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:15.770
I love that moment.

1500
01:05:15.950 --> 01:05:18.250
It made it intimate and funny and sweet.

1501
01:05:18.590 --> 01:05:22.430
I think it's time to talk about the greatest Dwight talking head of all time.

1502
01:05:22.430 --> 01:05:29.910
That's saying a lot because there are some amazing Dwight talking heads, but this one really is like top five.

1503
01:05:30.150 --> 01:05:32.230
He's going to describe his perfect crime.

1504
01:05:32.570 --> 01:05:34.230
And I think we have to hear it.

1505
01:05:35.230 --> 01:05:36.050
What is my perfect crime?

1506
01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:38.290
I break into Tiffany's in midnight.

1507
01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:39.950
Do I go for the vault?

1508
01:05:40.150 --> 01:05:41.630
No, I go for the chandelier.

1509
01:05:41.830 --> 01:05:42.370
It's priceless.

1510
01:05:42.870 --> 01:05:45.030
As I'm taking it down, a woman catches me.

1511
01:05:45.230 --> 01:05:46.250
She tells me to stop.

1512
01:05:46.490 --> 01:05:47.630
It's her father's business.

1513
01:05:47.850 --> 01:05:48.450
She's Tiffany.

1514
01:05:48.670 --> 01:05:50.770
I say, no, we make love all night.

1515
01:05:50.770 --> 01:05:53.850
In the morning, the cops come and I escape in one of their uniforMs.

1516
01:05:54.170 --> 01:05:56.750
I tell her to meet me in Mexico, but I go to Canada.

1517
01:05:56.970 --> 01:05:57.830
I don't trust her.

1518
01:05:58.230 --> 01:05:59.570
Besides, I like the cold.

1519
01:06:00.510 --> 01:06:01.930
Thirty years later, I get a postcard.

1520
01:06:02.130 --> 01:06:04.330
I have a son, and he's the chief of police.

1521
01:06:04.570 --> 01:06:06.070
This is where the story gets interesting.

1522
01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:09.510
I tell Tiffany to meet me in Paris by the Trocadero.

1523
01:06:10.410 --> 01:06:12.030
She's been waiting for me all these years.

1524
01:06:12.150 --> 01:06:13.250
